WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.678
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.678 --> 00:00:04.620
I helped some IoT
visionaries come to terms

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.090
with Load Balancer performance.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.252
Will their bet on the
future of technology payoff?

00:00:08.252 --> 00:00:09.210
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.860
IoT Roulette is a
company which offers

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.950
a scalable way for IoT
devices to connect with cloud

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.520
back ends through a common and
simple SDK and transport layer.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.227
A few weeks ago, I
met with their CEO

00:00:22.227 --> 00:00:24.060
to talk about the biggest
thing on her mind.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:28.020
What's the performance overhead
of using GCP's load balancer?

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.700
As you can imagine,
scale is really

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.470
important to IoT Roulette.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.900
With an estimated 50
billion IoT devices

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.360
in the world by 2020,
scaling your services

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.550
to handle the sudden load
of a million new devices

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:39.690
is an important feature.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.850
So fast performing
load balancers

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.560
are crucial to get this right.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.750
But before we start talking
about the balancers,

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.180
we need to see how
things perform directly.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.650
To get a baseline
performance, we

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.020
set up an instance in US
Central and kicked off

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.804
a bunch of cURL commands
from a machine in Europe.

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:56.220
Now, if you do
this test yourself,

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.303
you'll notice that the
cURL performance can end up

00:00:58.303 --> 00:01:00.810
being double what the
ping time is simply

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.200
due to the overhead of
TCP hand-shakes involved

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.310
and the transfer of
the fetched data.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:08.232
Now, Google Cloud Platform
provides two primary load

00:01:08.232 --> 00:01:09.690
balancers, so we
need to understand

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.250
the difference and profile
of each one individually.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.970
The first is a TCP UDP
load balancer, which

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.560
acts much like you'd expect.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.830
A request comes in from the
client to the load balancer

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.470
and is forwarded along
to the back end directly.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.970
The HTTP load balancer,
on the other hand,

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:27.150
has some serious magic going on.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.850
It proxies traffic through
Google front end servers

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.620
first, which stops the
session on the public internet

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.010
and continues on using
Google's private network.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.060
So depending on which
balancer you're using,

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.320
we should expect a different set
of performance characteristics.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.720
To test this, we set up instance
groups for each balancer

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.870
in the US Central region and
had a load generator in Europe

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.540
fetch from them using the
same cURL commands as before.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.060
The results show some
interesting characteristics.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.990
First, the TCP balancer
shows very similar results

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.940
to the direct access version.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.710
This is somewhat expected.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.780
In this case, traffic
moves from the user

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.330
to the load balancers, which
are in the region where

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:04.740
the VMs are located.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.810
As such, performance is
a factor of the distance

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:10.500
from the requester to the
closest entry in the target

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.380
region, just like
directly accessing the VM.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.220
The HTTP balancer,
on the other hand,

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.580
did something I really
wasn't expecting.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.050
According to this
test, it appears

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.630
to outperform both the
direct connection and the TCP

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:22.500
balancer.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.350
This is odd since the HTTP
load balancer effectively

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.150
adds another hop to each fetch.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.460
Instead of going from the
client to the balancer

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.740
to the back end, it has
the intermediate step

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.900
of hitting the front
end boxes first.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.570
Now, at first glance, this
seems confusing about why

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.940
adding an extra hop would
improve performance,

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.830
but once we look
under the hood, we

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.750
can quickly see why this works.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.360
See, when a request
hits the HTTP balancer,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.550
the TCP session stops there.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.760
The GFEs then move the request
on to the private network,

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.460
and all further interactions
happen between the GFE

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.019
and your specific back end.

00:02:55.019 --> 00:02:56.310
Now, here's the important part.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:59.190
After a GFE connects to a back
end to handle the request,

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:00.940
it keeps that connection open.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:02.970
This means that future
requests from this GFE

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.680
to this specific
back end will not

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.840
need the overhead of
creating a connection,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.787
and instead can actually
just go to sending data ASAP.

00:03:09.787 --> 00:03:12.120
The result of this setup means
that the first query that

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.990
causes the GFE to open a
connection to the back end

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:17.070
will see slightly
higher response times.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.050
Those are the spikes in
the front of the graph.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.420
However, subsequent packets
routed to the same back end

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.710
will see a lower
minimum latency.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.670
So for IoT Roulette,
this is fantastic.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.710
The more IoT devices
they get in a region,

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.440
the better their HTTP load
balancers will perform.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:32.939
Not a bad bet to make.

00:03:32.939 --> 00:03:34.730
If you'd like more
details on this problem,

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:36.896
check out the full article
linked in the description

00:03:36.896 --> 00:03:39.360
below, and make sure to
subscribe to our Google Cloud

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.940
YouTube channel to get alerts
when new performance videos

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:42.990
comes out.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.130
Because don't forget, when
it comes to performance,

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:46.410
every millisecond counts.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:51.860
